gin_availability) (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit) (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). So after we dispatch origin_availability_alpha, the dispatch slot becomes available again. Then we can allocate it to destination_availability_alpha. But goal also requires candidate_dispatch_slot_linked origin_availability_alpha dispatch_slot_alpha (still linked after dispatch?). The execute_origin_dispatch does not remove candidate_dispatch_slot_linked; effect does not mention removing that link. So after dispatch, the link remains? Actually effect does not include (not candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ...). So the link persists. And dispatch_slot_available becomes true again. So we can have both origin and destination availability linked to same slot? Not sure if allowed; but allocate action requires not dispatch_allocated ?candidate and dispatch_slot_available. After we allocate to origin, we have dispatch_allocated true, slot not available. After we dispatch origin, we get dispatch_slot_available true, but dispatch_allocated remains true? Execute_origin_dispatch does not change dispatch_allocated. So after dispatch, dispatch_allocated still true, slot available again? That seems odd but per definition: allocate sets dispatch_allocated true and slot not available. Execute_dispatch does not change dispatch_allocated, but makes slot available. So slot becomes available while still allocated? That seems inconsistent but maybe okay.)
